 "what": "Human skin reflectance for Digital Aerochrome's test surfaces, 10nm steps 300-1100nm.",
 "source": "Cooksey, C.C., Allen, D.W., Tsai, B.K., 'Reference Data Set of Human Skin Reflectance', Journal of Research of NIST 122:26 (2017), https://doi.org/10.6028/jres.122.026. Data: https://opendata.nist.gov/1832_Data_JResNIST_skinrefl%20v3.txt (NIST data, https://www.nist.gov/open/license). 100 subjects, directional-hemispherical reflectance factor, 250-2500nm in 3nm steps, each subject the mean of three measurements (the file's Average column).",
 "method": "Each subject's Average spectrum averaged into 10nm steps (the samples within +/-5nm); subjects ordered by mean reflectance over 500-650nm; 'lighter' is the pointwise median of the lightest third (34 subjects), 'medium' of the middle third (33), 'darker' of the darkest ten (500-650nm reflectance 0.16-0.32), so a single unusual reading cannot skew a tone. The dataset's subjects lean light, which is why the darker tone is the darkest ten rather than a third.",
